1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:03,840
Today I'm chatting with Noam Brown, who is a researcher at OpenAI.

2
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:10,400
He was one of the foundational contributors to what became O1 and the reasoning models, and now he's working on multi-agent systems.

3
00:00:10,670 --> 00:00:22,240
Speaking of which, you guys announced last week that you solved one of the Millennium Price problems with a system of 10,000 different AI agents that spent 130 billion tokens over 88 hours.

4
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:28,004
One of the reasons I'm interested in talking to you is I think you were in the first people maybe two, or three years ago,

5
00:00:28,430 --> 00:00:33,680
who was thinking about how the reasoning models would allow us to see into the future.

6
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:38,480
Because if you scale up inference compute, you can see what the base capabilities of the models will be a few years in the future.

7
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:47,760
And I feel like you're in a similar position now to help us understand what future capabilities will look like, given the enormous scaling of agent sizes that we can do right now.

8
00:00:48,174 --> 00:00:49,280
So the way I think about it,

9
00:00:50,286 --> 00:00:59,680
when you plot the performance of these reasoning models with test time compute on the x-axis and performance on basically any reasoning benchmark on the y-axis,

10
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:07,120
you see a very clear pattern where the longer these models take to think about their answer, the better they do. And this is like a very natural thing. It's the same thing with people.

11
00:01:07,598 --> 00:01:14,800
If you're taking the SATs, you have five minutes to go through the entire exam, you're not going to do very well. If you have five hours, you're probably going to do a lot better.

12
00:01:15,886 --> 00:01:22,000
The AI models are pretty similar, and they'll spend that time doing this monologue to themselves, figuring out, going through different cases,

13
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:26,240
ruling out different possibilities, building on some of their previous discoveries.

14
00:01:26,766 --> 00:01:30,080
The problem is that as you push that further and further, you hit a latency bottleneck.

15
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:33,572
You don't want to sit around for three years waiting for a response.

16
00:01:34,062 --> 00:01:38,400
And so what you can do is what a lot of people do is they parallelize, They just get a team of people.

17
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:42,640
if you're going to found a company, you want to get a group of people together so you can go faster.

18
00:01:42,958 --> 00:01:48,452
It's the same thing with these AI models that it helps to just have multiple agents working on something because they can just go faster.

19
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:54,564
And so multi-agent is a way of scaling test-time compute in parallel instead of purely serial.

20
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:03,844
And it is like less efficient because it doesn't have it's not like a single agent has all the context to itself, but it is like

21
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:08,000
a very like a very effective way of scaling test-time compute if it's done well.

22
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:15,120
Okay, I'm gonna ask a bunch of naive questions because these systems, so this is an unreleased model. So we haven't publicly seen how these systems work.

23
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:21,476
And so I just have a bunch of ways in which I'm like confused about like what the qualitative properties of such systems are.

24
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:31,040
I am shocked by the scale of cognitive effort that you can concentrate in such a short period of time.

25
00:02:31,374 --> 00:02:39,012
So if you think about what 130 billion tokens are, if it was a single human thinking as you know a full-time job stretched back to back,

26
00:02:39,406 --> 00:02:45,840
130 billion tokens would be a human thinking for like 4,000 years, you know, eight hours a day or something, working a normal work week.

27
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:58,160
So starting from like ancient Sumeria up till today, a single sequential human thinking that long, concentrated in 88 hours. I feel like qualitatively, that is a super important consideration.

28
00:02:58,350 --> 00:03:07,120
And I'm surprised that there isn't a bigger parallelization penalty, that you can just have 10,000 agents collaborate, and because maybe the agents are better collaborating than humans might be,

29
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:11,280
they're going much faster, that they can actually productively collaborate at such a big scale.

30
00:03:11,534 --> 00:03:16,320
Or maybe they, I don't know, maybe there is a big parallelization penalty. Yeah, let's talk about the parallelization penalty and then we can talk about the qualitative stuff.

31
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:21,120
Because the truth is that we don't have very good science on multi-agent scaling up to this kind of scale. Yeah.

32
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:25,600
So when we released 5.6, I think that was the first time that we had

33
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:34,960
a proper multi-agent system in uh in our models. And we actually did in the blog post show some plots of the scaling performance of multi-agent systems.

34
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:38,116
Because we we have it as an option, uh, it's ultra mode.

35
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:41,040
And the default is four agents, but you can set that to higher.

36
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:46,800
And in the plot, we show okay, here's what the performance looks like on some benchmarks for one agent, for four agents working together, for 16 agents working together.

37
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:56,388
And What you see, and it depends on the benchmark, but but for some of the benchmarks, basically if you have four agents working on the problem, it is done twice as fast.

38
00:03:56,944 --> 00:04:05,092
So you're basically paying, because there's four agents working for half as long, you're paying a 2x more to get an answer twice as quickly.

39
00:04:05,550 --> 00:04:11,584
If you go to 16 agents, you see a similar pattern, it's like a little less efficient, but you continue to see that performance.

40
00:04:11,984 --> 00:04:18,224
Is it a linear serial-time speed up or a sublinear speed up as you increase the number of parallel agents?

41
00:04:18,544 --> 00:04:24,224
I would say it's slightly sublinear, though it does depend a lot on the problem.

42
00:04:24,944 --> 00:04:30,864
So math, for example, is quite paralyzable. Uh it's not the most paralyzable thing, but it is very paralyzable.

43
00:04:31,024 --> 00:04:36,944
I think web search, things like doing a deep research report where you have to look through a bunch of sources, that's extremely paralyzable.

44
00:04:37,264 --> 00:04:41,760
I suspect that something like writing a novel would be very unparalyzable.

45
00:04:42,158 --> 00:04:46,436
So you would probably not see a big benefit from having 10,000 agents working on a novel together.

46
00:04:47,470 --> 00:04:51,104
in the same way that you'd probably not have a big benefit from having 10,000 people work on a novel together.

47
00:04:51,984 --> 00:04:54,544
So the performance does depend on the domain.

48
00:04:54,830 --> 00:04:59,264
We do measure it up to 16 or so agents in our published blog posts.

49
00:05:00,366 --> 00:05:05,584
the problem is it's very hard to push that science to like 10,000 agents because it's just so expensive.

50
00:05:06,944 --> 00:05:16,544
You guys just did it over like. a weekend. but that's one data point. Like we don't know how long it would take a single agent to solve mock airstokes because we haven't done that experiment yet.

51
00:05:16,624 --> 00:05:22,384
Yeah, yeah, yeah. And and maybe we will, but I mean that's also only one data point, right? And we want to do if we want to do a thorough ablation. That's interesting.

52
00:05:22,544 --> 00:05:25,984
Um it's actually the experiments are just too expensive to go to that scale.

53
00:05:26,254 --> 00:05:34,436
Right. So we have to do some kind of like methodical science about like what happens when you go to like 64, 128, 256, or something, and get a sense of the behavior.

54
00:05:35,150 --> 00:05:40,224
But it's going to be very hard to push that all the way to like 10,000 and know for sure what was

55
00:05:41,678 --> 00:05:44,544
the benefit that we actually got for using 10,000 agents versus a thousand.

56
00:05:44,784 --> 00:05:44,944
Yeah.

57
00:05:45,294 --> 00:05:48,624
One thing I want to make clear is that the effort to get

58
00:05:49,518 --> 00:05:52,064
it's not like the effort to get a millennium prize problem.

59
00:05:52,590 --> 00:06:05,904
This was not due to multi-agent. I wouldn't even like attribute like 10% of the credits to multi-agent. Like the reality is we've trained a OpenAI has trained like a very powerful model.

60
00:06:07,104 --> 00:06:12,544
and we can get that model to operate over very long horizons, we can get it to think in parallel.

61
00:06:12,864 --> 00:06:18,784
but at its core, the reason why we're able to do this is because we just have a general purpose, very strong model.

62
00:06:19,344 --> 00:06:27,584
And I think things like multi-agents are flashy and are new, and that probably

63
00:06:27,854 --> 00:06:30,784
gets disproportionate credit for that reason.

64
00:06:31,086 --> 00:06:34,224
But the the core reason is like this is just a very powerful model.

65
00:06:35,024 --> 00:06:38,464
So the generalization is actually quite shocking to me.

66
00:06:38,798 --> 00:06:44,304
The US systems, I mean, we don't I don't know how these systems were trained, but presumably they were trained how RL training happens.

67
00:06:44,384 --> 00:06:47,904
You have a bunch of checkable synthetic problems, you do a bunch of RL against them.

68
00:06:48,144 --> 00:06:53,984
And nowhere in the training process, I'm guessing, was like the model solving anything as ambitious as a Millennium Prize problem.

69
00:06:54,254 --> 00:07:03,588
But the generalization was strong enough that like you could have this like much easier verifiable problems generalize to this much parallel effort on such a hard problem.

70
00:07:04,304 --> 00:07:08,612
I think that is true. first of all, we do train the model on very hard problems.

71
00:07:08,974 --> 00:07:14,980
So there is definitely a gap. we see if we train on some kinds of tasks, it's able to do tasks that are more ambitious than that.

72
00:07:15,662 --> 00:07:20,644
There is an interesting challenge that as the models become smarter and smarter,

73
00:07:21,774 --> 00:07:23,684
the kinds of questions we can ask them

74
00:07:24,878 --> 00:07:26,404
a lot of them are too easy.

75
00:07:27,264 --> 00:07:29,572
And um it's hard to challenge the model.

76
00:07:30,064 --> 00:07:33,732
And I do think that that's going to be an interesting. Like if I had to make

77
00:07:34,766 --> 00:07:38,116
an argument for why you might not see

78
00:07:38,830 --> 00:07:48,384
AIs, like LLMs go the same path as AlphaGo and AlphaZero and all these kinds of like gameplaying AIs, it it might be that this this kind of problem that in things like AlphaZero,

79
00:07:48,464 --> 00:07:53,380
where you have self-play, you have an infinite curriculum. You're always playing against an AI that's like equally strong.

80
00:07:53,744 --> 00:08:01,444
Whereas for things like training in LLM with reinforcement learning, at least the ways that are out there right now,

81
00:08:02,670 --> 00:08:05,572
you give the model a problem and you ask it to solve it.

82
00:08:06,064 --> 00:08:10,624
And if the problem is so easy that I can just solve it in a second, it's not really learning anything.

83
00:08:11,984 --> 00:08:12,304
So

84
00:08:13,264 --> 00:08:21,104
if we run out of problems to ask it that challenge it, then that is a plausible scenario where actually like, okay, it becomes much harder to make progress.

85
00:08:21,664 --> 00:08:24,548
Now, I do think there are ways around that.

86
00:08:24,864 --> 00:08:28,784
And um, so we haven't really hit that uh as a as a wall yet.

87
00:08:28,910 --> 00:08:32,384
Um and I think that if it ever became a serious problem, there would be ways around it.

88
00:08:32,558 --> 00:08:34,724
But it is like a plausible scenario. Yeah.

89
00:08:35,086 --> 00:08:43,024
Um and just for the audience, when when you're referring to like Alpha Go or Alpha Zero, you're saying like getting superhuman relatively fast after achieving human level performance. Yeah.

90
00:08:43,104 --> 00:08:45,984
I mean, if you look at the trajectory of game playing as like Go,

91
00:08:46,944 --> 00:08:56,944
they within a span of like a year went from beating a European chess champion, being like, I don't know, like number 50 in the world, to beating the world champion to being unimaginably

92
00:08:57,294 --> 00:08:59,104
orders of magnitude stronger than any human alive.

93
00:08:59,904 --> 00:09:00,224
And

94
00:09:00,942 --> 00:09:08,704
it's possible that domains like math we see a similar trajectory, but i think there is a very plausible scenario where actually that doesn't happen.

95
00:09:08,944 --> 00:09:09,424
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

96
00:09:09,744 --> 00:09:15,012
So I want to understand if in six months people will have access to multi-agent systems.

97
00:09:15,726 --> 00:09:20,304
How should one model what it is like to collaborate with or hire a multi-agent system?

98
00:09:20,718 --> 00:09:29,476
Yeah, I should start by talking about how these multi-agent systems actually work, which is, I think, a very different way than a lot of multi-agent systems in other AIs.

99
00:09:29,824 --> 00:09:30,144
So

100
00:09:31,438 --> 00:09:39,268
a lot of people that have approached multi-agents for things like LLMs tend to take this very scaffolded approach where, for example, there might be a

101
00:09:39,744 --> 00:09:46,864
coordinator agent that delegates work to a bunch of children and gives them a task and the children work on it and then return their answer.

102
00:09:47,824 --> 00:09:48,144
and

103
00:09:49,166 --> 00:09:52,544
this seems like a very sensible setup, very sensible scaffold. It definitely helps.

104
00:09:52,846 --> 00:09:55,264
But there are a bunch of limitations with these kinds of setups.

105
00:09:55,344 --> 00:10:02,624
So, for example, if in this setup you have a coordinator that's sending tasks to children, the children work on it and then return their answers.

106
00:10:02,958 --> 00:10:04,292
Well, what happens if

107
00:10:04,974 --> 00:10:07,748
two children are given similar tasks? Can they talk to each other?

108
00:10:08,046 --> 00:10:09,444
And usually the answer is no.

109
00:10:09,904 --> 00:10:11,584
and that's very inefficient, right?

110
00:10:12,064 --> 00:10:20,224
if you're given a task and it's actually really helpful to talk to somebody that might know an answer to a question that you're working on or a part of something that you're working on,

111
00:10:20,304 --> 00:10:23,744
it'd be really helpful if we'd just be able to ping them and say, hey, can you help me out with this thing?

112
00:10:23,984 --> 00:10:30,384
But a lot of systems don't have that set up, and adding it just increases the complexity significantly to the scaffold that you have.

113
00:10:30,670 --> 00:10:32,484
Another thing is like, what if the child

114
00:10:33,230 --> 00:10:35,908
doesn't really understand or has a clarification question?

115
00:10:36,464 --> 00:10:44,784
So it has to choose then between, okay, do I just return and ask the question instead of giving instead of solving the problem, or

116
00:10:45,104 --> 00:10:52,484
do I solve the problem, just like make an assumption about what the parent wanted me to do and uh and just solve it that way.

117
00:10:54,510 --> 00:11:00,676
so in any scaffold that people come up with, there's always limitations involved. And the approach that we wanted to take was to just

118
00:11:01,326 --> 00:11:09,584
go toward the extreme end of baking in as little structure as we could and give the agents very primitive tools to use and

119
00:11:09,744 --> 00:11:11,824
figure out for themselves how to use it effectively.

120
00:11:12,142 --> 00:11:16,464
So we give the agents the ability to message another agent, and

121
00:11:17,390 --> 00:11:20,704
when it messages another agent, it is inserted into the context. And

122
00:11:21,454 --> 00:11:23,460
then it can do with like a few other similar things.

123
00:11:23,744 --> 00:11:29,824
but that's basically the core of it, that it can just send a message whenever it wants, just a tool call,

124
00:11:30,734 --> 00:11:35,104
and it can send that to other agents. And they figure out for themselves the best way to coordinate around that.

125
00:11:35,344 --> 00:11:43,268
And it turns out that if this is done well, you get very sophisticated behavior. And to me, it looks a lot like

126
00:11:44,224 --> 00:11:47,824
how human collaborators work over something like Slack, for example.

127
00:11:48,238 --> 00:11:50,564
When we were working on this project, it was really exciting

128
00:11:51,502 --> 00:11:56,464
when we finally got it working to see these agents working on problems together. I remember one example.

129
00:11:56,686 --> 00:12:01,344
So we give the agents a problem, and then one agent says, I think I've got the answer.

130
00:12:01,504 --> 00:12:08,784
And then another agent says, Actually, I got a different answer. And then they have this whole discussion about, well, how did you arrive at that answer? Can you explain it to me?

131
00:12:08,944 --> 00:12:17,104
And going back and forth and trying to clarify like what could have been wrong in each other's reasoning. And then they finally converge on, like, oh yeah, okay, that seems right.

132
00:12:17,264 --> 00:12:21,604
And then it just broadcast to the other agents, like, actually, I've changed my answer. I think, I think he's right.

133
00:12:22,384 --> 00:12:25,572
And it just felt like a very natural

134
00:12:26,510 --> 00:12:27,268
conversation.

135
00:12:27,824 --> 00:12:28,144
Um,

136
00:12:29,104 --> 00:12:34,704
it's kind of felt like when you see chain of thought for the first time that's trained through reinforcement learning, and you're like, oh,

137
00:12:35,184 --> 00:12:40,704
this is just kind of like what a person would think if they were writing down their thoughts as they're thinking them.

138
00:12:40,944 --> 00:12:42,116
It kind of felt like that.

139
00:12:42,958 --> 00:12:50,224
So it is it is really cool to see this kind of behavior. And so I think collaborating with these things, honestly, it feels a lot like collaborating with a person. Yeah. So it's a very natural flow.

140
00:12:50,544 --> 00:12:55,824
Except one qualitative difference that might become salient in the future is that

141
00:12:57,264 --> 00:13:04,624
These systems will be thinking, I don't know, more than 10x as fast, right? If you just look at how many tokens per second they output versus how fast a human talks.

142
00:13:04,974 --> 00:13:07,364
And they're working all the time. They're not sleeping.

143
00:13:07,758 --> 00:13:12,784
And they're collaborating with each other at a much more intense pace than humans have the capacity to collaborate with other humans. So

144
00:13:14,224 --> 00:13:17,924
I'm trying to think of what to qualitatively expect in a year.

145
00:13:18,446 --> 00:13:25,024
And is it like a sort of shadow organization that is moving 100x faster in my company than the human level is?

146
00:13:25,262 --> 00:13:28,704
This, you know, like the the iteration cycle is much faster.

147
00:13:29,424 --> 00:13:35,824
What would take a human organization a year to do is happening within a week within this like uh the shadow organization.

148
00:13:36,334 --> 00:13:43,104
So will it feel foreign? I don't know. I've actually found that it's pretty surprisingly natural to work with these things right now.

149
00:13:43,310 --> 00:13:51,104
I think that could change. So for example, we have these like ultra fast modes that enable sampling to be like 10 or 15x faster or whatever.

150
00:13:51,264 --> 00:13:53,664
And then like, okay, it's gonna be pretty hard to keep up with these things.

151
00:13:54,766 --> 00:14:00,304
I think the idea is these agents, when they're communicating with each other, yeah, they can go super fast,

152
00:14:00,494 --> 00:14:03,504
but then also they understand when they're talking to an agent versus when they're talking to a person.

153
00:14:03,918 --> 00:14:14,144
And their behavior will be different in those situations. So the main example that we have publicly of sophisticated multi-agent systems is unfortunately the hugging face one.

154
00:14:14,304 --> 00:14:16,304
And the thing I found interesting there, I mean,

155
00:14:17,486 --> 00:14:24,384
a lot of things I found concerning, obviously, but the thing I found interesting is just like the spontaneous emergence of hierarchy of like middle management.

156
00:14:24,814 --> 00:14:30,064
And it sounds like you're saying like this level of organization sort of emerges spontaneously from training.

157
00:14:30,544 --> 00:14:34,276
I think the details are spontaneous, but I mean, just because we're

158
00:14:35,278 --> 00:14:43,264
giving a lot of flexibility to the agents to decide how to communicate with each other in the optimal way, it doesn't mean like we are still giving them a starting point.

159
00:14:43,344 --> 00:14:51,264
We're giving them a prior about, oh, this is what reasonable communication might look like. They also I mean they're trained on a lot of human text.

